{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["<b>On the eighth day.</b> And it does not say 'and on the day' — to teach you that it is a festival by itself." "<b>Atzeret it shall be for you.</b> Therefore the eighth day of the festival is called Atzeret, and for this reason Shavuot is likewise called Atzeret, as it is written about it <b>בשבעותיכם</b>, and we translate it <b>בעצרותיכון</b>. But the seventh day of Pesach, concerning which it is not written 'atzeret for you' but rather 'for HaShem your Elohim' — it is not called Atzeret." "<b>On the eighth day, atzeret it shall be for you.</b> If so, why did the sages permit taking leave of the sukka on the last festival day? Rather, all seven days of the festival they would pray for dew, and on the last day they would pray for rain; therefore they take leave of the sukka, so that they may pray with a whole heart for the rains."], :book :numbers, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:20:15.351997137Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}